    "points": 3,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "How many different triangles of area 1 can be drawn if each of their angles may only be equal to $40^\\circ$, $50^\\circ$, $100^\\circ$, or $90^\\circ$? (Congruent triangles are considered the same.)",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "0"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "4"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "6"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Possible angle triples",
        "body": "The three angles must add up to $180^\\circ$ using only $40, 50, 90, 100$.\n- With $100^\\circ$: the other two add to $80^\\circ$, so $40 + 40$.\n- With $90^\\circ$ (and no $100^\\circ$): the other two add to $90^\\circ$, so $40 + 50$.\n- Without $90^\\circ$ or $100^\\circ$: the angles are from $\\{40, 50\\}$ and sum to at most $150^\\circ$ — impossible.\n\nSo the shapes are $(100^\\circ, 40^\\circ, 40^\\circ)$ and $(90^\\circ, 50^\\circ, 40^\\circ)$. For each shape, the area fixes the size (scaling by $k$ multiplies the area by $k^2$), so there is exactly one triangle of area 1 of each shape: 2 triangles.\n\n**Answer: (C) 2**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry",
      "counting"
    ],
    "difficulty": 1,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
  },
