    },
    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 4,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "$ABCD$ is a square of side 1. $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ are centres of quarter-circles that intersect at $P$, $Q$, $R$ and $S$, as shown in the drawing. What is the size of $\\angle AQB$?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2015-g0910/q20.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$90^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$105^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$120^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$135^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$150^\\circ$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Equilateral triangle",
        "body": "$Q$ lies on the quarter-circles centred at $C$ and $D$ (radius 1), so $QC = QD = CD = 1$: triangle $QCD$ is equilateral and $\\angle QDC = 60^\\circ$. Hence $\\angle ADQ = 90^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 30^\\circ$.\n\nTriangle $ADQ$ is isosceles with $DA = DQ = 1$, so $\\angle DAQ = \\angle DQA = \\frac{180^\\circ - 30^\\circ}{2} = 75^\\circ$, and $\\angle QAB = 90^\\circ - 75^\\circ = 15^\\circ$. By symmetry $\\angle QBA = 15^\\circ$ too, so\n$$\\angle AQB = 180^\\circ - 15^\\circ - 15^\\circ = 150^\\circ.$$\n\n**Answer: (E) $150^\\circ$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Coordinates",
        "body": "Put $B = (0,0)$, $C = (1,0)$, $D = (1,1)$, $A = (0,1)$. The point at distance 1 from both $C$ and $D$ near side $AB$ is $Q = (1 - \\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}, \\frac12)$. The half-angle at $Q$ in the isosceles triangle $AQB$ satisfies\n$$\\tan\\tfrac{\\angle AQB}{2} = \\frac{1/2}{1 - \\sqrt3/2} = \\frac{1}{2 - \\sqrt3} = 2 + \\sqrt3 = \\tan 75^\\circ,$$\nso $\\angle AQB = 150^\\circ$.\n\n**Answer: (E) $150^\\circ$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
