    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "We are interested in sequences $a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots$ that satisfy the following property: $a_{n+2} = a_{n+1} + a_n$, where $a_m$ is a positive integer for any $m$, and it is known that $a_7 = 2015$. How many such sequences exist?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "0"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "5"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "50"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "Infinitely many"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Express $a_7$ through $a_1, a_2$",
        "body": "Let $a_1 = x$, $a_2 = y$. Then $a_3 = x + y$, $a_4 = x + 2y$, $a_5 = 2x + 3y$, $a_6 = 3x + 5y$, $a_7 = 5x + 8y$. The sequence is fixed by $x, y$, and all terms are positive integers exactly when $x, y$ are.\n\nSo we count positive integer solutions of $5x + 8y = 2015$. Since $5 \\mid 2015$, $5 \\mid 8y$, so $y = 5k$ and $x = 403 - 8k$. We need $k \\ge 1$ and $403 - 8k \\ge 1$, i.e. $k \\le 50$. That gives 50 sequences.\n\n**Answer: (D) 50**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "sequences-series",
